\section{Why a second pair of registries}{


Each of these two registries is an \strong{exact partition} of its address space
-- 256 IPv4 \verb{/8}s and 20 IPv6 blocks that tile \verb{::/0} with no gap and no
overlap, asserted at build time. Together with \code{\link[=addr_registry]{addr_registry()}} that makes
classification total: every address matches some row, so "globally
reachable ordinary unicast" becomes a statement backed by a registry row
rather than an inference from an absence.

Classifying from the special-purpose registries alone is a known
CVE-producing pattern, and the clearest case is \strong{multicast}: neither
special-purpose registry contains \verb{224.0.0.0/4} or \verb{ff00::/8} at all. A
table derived from them therefore has no multicast handling, which is how
\code{ssrfcheck} shipped CVE-2025-8267.
}

\section{Precedence, stated by the source}{


The special-purpose registries \strong{outrank} these. That is not raddr's
judgment: the address-space registries themselves carry "For authoritative
registration, see [Special-Purpose Address Space]".

Because these two are exact partitions, \emph{every} special-purpose block falls
inside one of their rows, so precedence is not an edge case -- it decides
every lookup that matches both layers. Five prefixes appear in both pairs
\strong{identically} (\verb{0.0.0.0/8}, \verb{10.0.0.0/8}, \verb{127.0.0.0/8}, \verb{fc00::/7},
\verb{fe80::/10}); for those the two layers agree and only the columns differ.
}

\section{No policy columns, and none invented}{


The five IANA policy logicals do not exist in these registries, so they are
absent here rather than filled in. The special-purpose registry answers
policy; the address-space registry answers identity; neither invents the
other's answer.

What these carry instead is \code{status} (IPv4: \code{ALLOCATED}, \code{LEGACY} or
\code{RESERVED}; \code{NA} for IPv6, which has no such column), \code{date} (IPv4 only) and
\code{notes} (IPv6 only -- free prose, and the only record that \verb{200::/7} and
\verb{fec0::/10} are deprecated).

\code{rfc} is \code{NA} for \strong{every} IPv4 row, because that registry has no reference
column. The RFCs behind its rows live in numeric footnotes whose text is on
the registry page and not in the CSV, so raddr reports the marker in
\code{footnotes} and does not transcribe the citation.
